  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_53",
      "text": "Asks whether a rhythm is stronger than you would expect from a series with the same character but no real rhythm. The catch it exists to solve: shuffling the values destroys the smooth run-to-run structure that every biological recording has, so a shuffled null is far too easy to beat and almost anything looks rhythmic. The block bootstrap instead resamples whole chunks (a day at a time by default) and the AR(1) option simulates matched \"red noise\", so both keep that structure. Phase-randomised and AAFT surrogates are offered too, but they preserve the spectrum — and therefore the rhythm itself — so they cannot test for rhythmicity; the node says so when you pick one. Results are reproducible from the seed, and the smallest p it can report is 1/(surrogates + 1).",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 24,
      "width": 360,
      "height": 190
    }
  ],
